# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) to make the LIVE opencode e2e reproducible on this machine.
# vitest.setup.ts loads .env.local before any test; shell vars override these (shell-WINS).
#
# These ONLY configure the live e2e suite (src/host/rc/opencode/driver.e2e.test.ts). With none set it
# targets a local `opencode serve` on the default port + the cheap ollama model, and SKIPS when the
# server is unreachable — so unit tests and CI stay green with no setup.

# The opencode server the e2e attaches to (default http://127.0.0.1:4096).
OPENCODE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4096

# The model the e2e drives, as "providerID/modelID". The default (ollama/qwen2.5:0.5b) is too small to
# reliably call tools, so the tool + permission round-trip scenarios SKIP. Point at a tool-capable model
# to exercise them fully — e.g. Bedrock Haiku (proven):
#   RC_OPENCODE_E2E_MODEL=amazon-bedrock/us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0
# The PROVIDER must be configured + authenticated on the opencode SERVER process itself, NOT here — e.g.
# for Bedrock the server needs AWS_REGION + credentials (env keys, AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK, or an
# instance role) in ITS environment. These test vars never reach the server; they only tell the e2e
# which server/model to drive.
RC_OPENCODE_E2E_MODEL=amazon-bedrock/us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0

# Per-live-turn budget in ms for a slow/contended box (default 120000). A turn that exceeds it SKIPS
# (never fails) — environmental slowness is not a code bug.
# RC_OPENCODE_E2E_TURN_MS=120000
